CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Act is to prevent the social isolation of children and young adults and improve their quality of life by prescribing matters concerning support for children and young adults in crisis, and to enhance the vitality of society as a whole by ensuring that they lead lives worthy of human dignity and participate in society in a healthy manner as members of society.

Article 2 (Definitions)
The terms used in this Act are defined as follows:
1. The term "children and young adults in crisis" means persons aged 34 or younger who fall under any of the following: Provided, That, for children and young adults providing family care under item (a) who are fulfilling or have fulfilled military service obligations under the Military Service Act, the period of military service fulfilled shall be added to the age limit:
(a) Children and young adults providing family care: Persons providing nursing, caregiving, management of daily activities, or other assistance to family members who require care;
(b) Children and young adults in social isolation or withdrawal: Persons who have considerable difficulty in daily life because they have almost no interaction with others or live only within a limited residential space for a substantial period or longer;
(c) Other persons in a crisis situation prescribed by Presidential Decree;
2. The term "social security benefits" means cash, goods, services, and vouchers for their use provided by the State and local governments pursuant to Article 3, subparagraph 1 of the Framework Act on Social Security;
3. The term "case management" means support that links and provides social security benefits and private-sector services on a basis tailored to each individual through the support system under this Act;
4. The term "children and young adults in need of assistance" means children and young adults in crisis who wish to receive case management;
5. The term "persons eligible for support" means children and young adults in need of assistance who have been selected for case management pursuant to Article 13;
6. The term "dedicated support organization" means an institution, organization, or corporation designated and entrusted by the State or a local government pursuant to Article 22 to perform duties dedicated to supporting children and young adults in crisis.

Article 3 (Basic Principles for Supporting Children and Young Adults in Crisis)
(1) In providing support to children and young adults in crisis, the interests of the children and young adults shall be given the highest priority.
(2) Children and young adults in crisis shall not be subjected to discrimination of any kind in connection with support under this Act on the grounds of their own or their parents' sex, age, religion, social status, property, disability status, place of birth, race, or other characteristics.
(3) Children and young adults in crisis shall have the right to receive support under this Act to guarantee their rights and promote their welfare.

Article 4 (Responsibilities of the State and Local Governments)
(1) The State and local governments shall take necessary measures, including surveys, research, education, and publicity, to improve social awareness of children and young adults in crisis and enable them to become self-reliant as autonomous members of society.
(2) The State and local governments shall endeavor to identify children and young adults in crisis at an early stage and shall establish and implement the legal and institutional arrangements necessary to support them.

Article 5 (Relationship with Other Acts)
Support for children and young adults in crisis shall be governed by this Act, except as otherwise specially provided in other Acts.

CHAPTER II ESTABLISHMENT OF MASTER PLANS FOR SUPPORTING CHILDREN AND YOUNG ADULTS IN CRISIS

Article 6 (Establishment and Implementation of Master Plans for Supporting Children and Young Adults in Crisis)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare shall establish and implement, every five years, a master plan for supporting children and young adults in crisis (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan") that includes the following matters:
1. Basic objectives and directions for implementation;
2. Measures for identifying children and young adults in crisis and providing tailored integrated services;
3. Measures for surveys, research, education, publicity, and institutional improvements to support children and young adults in crisis;
4. Measures for developing professional personnel under Article 25 (2) 4;
5. Measures for securing financial resources necessary to implement the master plan;
6. Other matters necessary to support children and young adults in crisis.
(2) The Minister of Health and Welfare shall establish and implement the master plan in conformity with the master plan for child policy under Article 7 of the Child Welfare Act and the master plan for young adult policy under Article 8 of the Framework Act on Young Adults.
(3) When establishing or amending the master plan, the Minister of Health and Welfare shall obtain deliberation by the Child Policy Coordination Committee under Article 10 of the Child Welfare Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Child Policy Coordination Committee") and the Young Adult Policy Coordination Committee under Article 13 of the Framework Act on Young Adults (hereinafter referred to as the "Young Adult Policy Coordination Committee").
(4) Other matters necessary for the establishment, amendment, implementation, and other aspects of the master plan shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 7 (Establishment and Implementation of Annual Implementation Plans)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, and the heads of local governments shall establish and implement annual implementation plans for supporting children and young adults in crisis (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plans") every year in accordance with the master plan.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments shall submit to the Minister of Health and Welfare each year their implementation plans for the following year and their performance under the implementation plans for the previous year.
(3) The Minister of Health and Welfare shall evaluate the performance submitted by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments pursuant to paragraph (2), establish the implementation plan for the following year by reflecting the results thereof, and report it to the Child Policy Coordination Committee and the Young Adult Policy Coordination Committee.
(4) Other matters necessary for the establishment, amendment, implementation, and other aspects of implementation plans shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 8 (Fact-Finding Surveys)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare shall conduct a fact-finding survey on children and young adults in crisis (hereinafter referred to as a "fact-finding survey") every three years for the purpose of establishing the master plan and implementation plans and shall publish the results thereof.
(2) Where children or young adults in need of assistance are identified during a fact-finding survey, the Minister of Health and Welfare may provide their telephone numbers (including mobile telephone numbers; hereinafter the same shall apply) to a dedicated support organization with the consent of the persons concerned.
(3) Matters necessary for the contents and methods of fact-finding surveys, publication of their results, and provision of the telephone numbers of children and young adults in need of assistance under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 9 (Cooperation in Establishing Plans and Conducting Fact-Finding Surveys)
Where necessary to establish and implement the master plan and implementation plans or conduct fact-finding surveys, the Minister of Health and Welfare may request relevant central administrative agencies, public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, and other relevant corporations and organizations (hereinafter referred to as "institutions, etc." in this Article) to cooperate, including by submitting materials or presenting opinions. In such cases, institutions, etc. requested to cooperate shall comply unless there is good cause not to do so.

Chapter 3 Procedures for Supporting Children and Young Adults in Crisis

Article 10 (Identification of Children and Young Adults in Need of Assistance)
(1) The head of a dedicated support organization may, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, conduct counseling for case management on the head's own initiative for a child or young adult in crisis who falls under any of the following subparagraphs. In such cases, Article 12(2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to matters concerning the content of the counseling.
1. A person who has been identified as a child or young adult in need of assistance during a fact-finding survey and has consented to the provision of that person's telephone number to the dedicated support organization;
2. A person whom any of the following persons, in the course of performing their duties, has identified as a child or young adult in need of assistance and for whom that person has requested support from the dedicated support organization:
(a) Teaching staff under Article 19 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act and Article 14 of the Higher Education Act;
(b) The head of a medical institution under Article 3 of the Medical Service Act;
(c) The head of an institution providing integrated services to children in vulnerable groups under Article 37 of the Child Welfare Act;
(d) The head of a child welfare facility under Article 50 of the Child Welfare Act;
(e) The head of a juvenile welfare facility under Article 17 of the Framework Act on Juveniles;
(f) The head of a social welfare facility under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Social Welfare Services Act;
(g) The head of a facility for young adults under subparagraph 7 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Young Adults;
(h) The head of a juvenile counseling and welfare center under Article 29 of the Juvenile Welfare Support Act, the head of a support center for juveniles with a migration background under Article 30 of that Act, and the head of a juvenile shelter under subparagraph 1 of Article 31 of that Act;
(i) The head of a support center for out-of-school juveniles under Article 12 of the Act on Support for Out-of-School Juveniles;
(j) The head of any other institution deemed necessary by the Minister of Health and Welfare in connection with the identification of children and young adults in need of assistance.
(2) The Minister of Health and Welfare may, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, establish and operate one-stop support channels for children and young adults in crisis, including online and telephone channels, for identifying children and young adults in need of assistance, providing counseling, receiving applications, and other purposes.

Article 11 (Application for Case Management)
(1) A child or young adult in need of assistance, or a relative living with that person (meaning a blood relative within the eighth degree of kinship; hereinafter the same shall apply), may apply to a dedicated support organization for case management, either directly or on behalf of the child or young adult in need of assistance.
(2) Matters necessary for the methods and procedures for applications under paragraph (1) and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 12 (Counseling)
(1) Upon receiving an application under Article 11, the head of a dedicated support organization shall conduct counseling through methods such as telephone or online counseling or a visit to the residence, in order to ascertain the circumstances of the child or young adult in need of assistance.
(2) Counseling under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
1. Personal details, household characteristics, and the living environment of the child or young adult in need of assistance;
2. The current status of the receipt of social security benefits by the household of the child or young adult in need of assistance;
3. Where the child or young adult in need of assistance is a child or young adult providing family care, the time spent caring for family members and the household's main source of income;
4. Whether the child or young adult in need of assistance satisfies the requirements under the subparagraphs of Article 13(1);
5. Other matters determined by the Minister of Health and Welfare.
(3) Where, as a result of counseling under paragraph (1), the head of a dedicated support organization determines that a child or young adult in need of assistance qualifies as a person eligible for support as a child or young adult providing family care, but the family member who needs care does not satisfy the requirement under item (a) of subparagraph 1 of Article 13(1), the head may apply for social security benefits necessary to satisfy that requirement on behalf of the child or young adult in need of assistance or that person's family. In such cases, the head of the dedicated support organization shall obtain the consent of the child or young adult in need of assistance or that person's family.
(4) Where, as a result of counseling under paragraph (1), a child or young adult in need of assistance is suspected of being a person with mental illness under subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Act on the Promotion of Mental Health and Support for Welfare Services for Persons with Mental Illness, the head of a dedicated support organization may inform that person's family of that fact and provide guidance on receiving counseling at a relevant specialized institution prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(5) Specific matters necessary for the procedures and methods for counseling under paragraph (1), applications under paragraph (3), and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 13 (Selection of Persons Eligible for Support, Etc.)
(1) The head of a dedicated support organization shall select a child or young adult in need of assistance who has been identified or has applied under Articles 10 through 12 as a person eligible for family care support if all the requirements in the items of subparagraph 1 are satisfied, or as a person eligible for support for social isolation or withdrawal if all the requirements in the items of subparagraph 2 are satisfied, and shall inform that person or that person's family of the result, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
1. Children and young adults providing family care:
(a) There shall be a family member requiring care (meaning a relative specified by public notice of the Minister of Health and Welfare who has difficulty maintaining daily life without the assistance of others owing to advanced age, disability, illness, major surgery, mental illness, drugs, or other reasons, such as a beneficiary under the Long-Term Care Insurance Act; hereinafter the same shall apply);
(b) There shall be no other family member aged 35 years or older apart from the family member requiring care and the child or young adult in need of assistance. However, this shall not apply where the child or young adult may be regarded as a child or young adult providing family care, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, even if there is another family member aged 35 years or older;
(c) The addresses of the family member requiring care and the child or young adult in need of assistance under the Resident Registration Act shall be the same. However, this shall not apply where the family member requiring care and the child or young adult in need of assistance may be regarded as sharing a livelihood, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare;
(d) The child or young adult in need of assistance shall be experiencing considerable difficulties in their studies and in finding employment owing to family care.
2. Children and young adults in social isolation or withdrawal:
(a) The head of the dedicated support organization shall consider it appropriate to select the person as eligible for support, taking into comprehensive consideration the results of counseling under Article 12, the person's willingness to participate in programs under Article 21, and other relevant matters;
(b) The person shall fall within cases prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, including those based on the results of applying the standardized scale under Article 20.
(2) Other matters necessary for the requirements applicable to cases under item (c) of subparagraph 1 of Article 2, the selection of persons eligible for support, and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 14 (Preparation of Case Management Plans, Etc.)
(1) The head of a dedicated support organization shall prepare a case management plan for each person eligible for support that includes the following matters, and shall continuously manage the case until case management is terminated so that necessary social security benefits can be provided in accordance with that plan:
1. The results of counseling under Article 12;
2. Preparation of future career plans and related counseling;
3. Support under Articles 15 through 19 and the provision of programs tailored to children and young adults in social isolation or withdrawal under Article 21(1);
4. Public and private services in the fields of housing, finance, legal matters, education (including public and private scholarships), health, and employment to which the dedicated support organization can provide referrals;
5. Other social security benefits for which the household of the person eligible for support is eligible;
6. Where the person eligible for support is a child or young adult providing family care, additional support, such as public and private services in the fields of caregiving, nursing, and medical care for the family member requiring care;
7. Other resources available for referral within the local community in which the dedicated support organization is located.
(2) Where a person eligible for support is a child or young adult providing family care, the head of a dedicated support organization may apply, on behalf of that person or the family member requiring care, for the social security benefits and public and private services included in the case management plan under paragraph (1), in order to facilitate the coordination and provision of services. In such cases, the head shall obtain the consent of the person eligible for support or at least one member of that person's family.
(3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), the head of a dedicated support organization shall not apply on behalf of another person for services that entail a financial burden or liability for the person eligible for support or that person's family, such as loans or contractual transactions.
(4) Where the head of a dedicated support organization considers it appropriate to terminate case management for a person eligible for support on grounds such as the achievement of the objective of self-reliance or a change in circumstances concerning the requirements under Article 13(1), the head may decide to terminate case management. In such cases, the head shall hear the views of the person eligible for support.
(5) Matters necessary for the detailed preparation of case management plans under paragraph (1) and the procedures therefor, decisions on termination under paragraph (4), and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Chapter 4 Provision of and Linkage to Social Security Benefits

Article 15 (Support for Psychological Counseling, Etc.)
(1) The State and local governments shall provide psychological counseling services to deliver support suited to the wishes and needs of persons eligible for support, and shall endeavor to ensure that such services can be provided in coordination with other services.
(2) The State and local governments shall provide psychological and emotional support to persons eligible for support through group programs, the operation of self-help groups, and other means.
(3) When providing psychological counseling services under paragraph (1), the head of a dedicated support organization shall also connect persons eligible for support and their families to those services.

Article 16 (Support for Health Management)
(1) The State and local governments shall endeavor to ensure that persons eligible for support can receive various health management services, including nutrition and health education, health checkups, mental health examinations, and medical care.
(2) Where needed based on the results of providing services under paragraph (1), the head of a dedicated support organization shall connect persons eligible for support with support for medical expenses.
(3) The criteria and procedures for support for medical expenses under paragraph (2) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 17 (Educational and Employment Support)
(1) The State and local governments shall endeavor to ensure that persons eligible for support can receive educational and employment support services suited to their aptitudes and abilities.
(2) When providing educational and employment support services under paragraph (1), the head of a dedicated support organization shall coordinate those services with private and public scholarships, vocational training programs, and other relevant programs.

Article 18 (Housing Support)
The State and local governments may give persons eligible for support priority in the provision of public rental housing, as prescribed by the Special Act on Public Housing, in order to create a stable living environment for them.

Article 19 (Special Support for Children and Young Adults Providing Family Care)
(1) The State and local governments may pay self-care allowances to children and young adults providing family care among persons eligible for support, to help cover the costs necessary to prepare for their own future.
(2) The State and local governments may provide the following special support to promote the self-reliance of children and young adults providing family care among persons eligible for support:
1. Where the family member requiring care is a recipient of long-term care benefits under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Long-Term Care Insurance Act, support for the use of institutional care benefits, notwithstanding Article 3(3) of that Act. However, this shall not apply to recipients prescribed by Presidential Decree according to the criteria for determining grades under Article 15(2) of that Act;
2. Where the household of the person eligible for support receives services under the Act on the Use of Social Services and Management of Vouchers or other services prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare, additional support, such as reductions in copayment rates;
3. Where the household of the person eligible for support is a beneficiary under the National Basic Living Security Act, deferral of the requirement to participate in self-support programs under Article 9(5) of that Act, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare;
4. Other support through social security benefits prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
(3) Matters necessary for the types of social security benefits under paragraphs (1) and (2), detailed selection criteria, methods, procedures, and timing of payment, and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 20 (Development of Scientific Scales for Social Isolation or Withdrawal)
The State and local governments shall develop and disseminate standardized scales and indicators so that signs of social isolation or withdrawal among children and young adults in crisis, the degree thereof, etc. can be identified on the basis of objective and scientific evidence.

Article 21 (Provision and Operation of Tailored Programs for Children and Young Adults in Social Isolation or Withdrawal)
(1) The State and local governments shall provide and operate tailored programs in which persons eligible for support can participate, in order to implement the case management plans established by the heads of dedicated support organizations pursuant to Article 14.
(2) Where necessary to provide and operate tailored programs under paragraph (1), the State and local governments may require persons eligible for support who participate in the relevant programs to bear a portion of the costs of providing and operating such programs.
(3) Matters necessary concerning the scope of programs requiring participants to bear a portion of the costs under paragraph (2), the criteria for calculating such contributions, the contribution rates, etc. shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

CHAPTER 5 ESTABLISHMENT OF A FOUNDATION FOR SUPPORT FOR CHILDREN AND YOUNG ADULTS IN CRISIS

Article 22 (Designation of Dedicated Support Organizations and Entrustment of Duties)
(1) In order to perform the duties dedicated to supporting children and young adults in crisis under Articles 10 through 21, the State and local governments may, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, designate institutions, organizations, or corporations recognized as having the ability to perform such duties as dedicated support organizations and entrust them with such duties.
(2) The State and local governments may evaluate the performance of duties by dedicated support organizations under paragraph (1), and may revoke their designation or the entrustment of duties to them according to the results of such evaluations.
(3) The State and local governments may provide financial assistance, within budgetary limits, for the expenses necessary for the operation of dedicated support organizations and the performance of their duties.
(4) Matters necessary concerning the designation of dedicated support organizations and the entrustment of duties to them, the revocation of such designation or entrustment, the frequency of evaluations of dedicated support organizations, budgetary assistance, etc. under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 23 (Use of the Social Security Information System)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may grant the heads of dedicated support organizations authority to access and process information in the social security information system under Article 37 of the Framework Act on Social Security (hereinafter referred to as the "social security information system"), to the extent necessary for the efficient processing of various materials and information needed to perform duties under Articles 10 through 21 and for the computerization of recordkeeping and management work.
(2) Matters necessary concerning the scope, methods, procedures, etc. for accessing and processing information under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 24 (Provision and Use of Data on Children and Young Adults in Crisis, Etc.)
(1) Where necessary to identify children and young adults in crisis, the Minister of Health and Welfare may request information falling under any of the following subparagraphs (hereinafter referred to as "public data") from relevant public institutions, and may collect, manage, and retain such information through the social security information system within the scope of the purpose for which it was provided:
1. The student's personal details, attendance records, behavioral characteristics, and overall assessments among the school records under Article 25 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act;
2. Records of receipt of unemployment benefits under Article 37 of the Employment Insurance Act by persons aged 19 through 34;
3. Records of payment of health insurance premiums under the National Health Insurance Act for the most recent six months;
4. Resident registration data under the Resident Registration Act;
5. Other information prescribed by Presidential Decree for identifying children and young adults in crisis.
(2) Where public data collected pursuant to paragraph (1) indicate that a child or young adult may be in crisis, the Minister of Health and Welfare may, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, provide personal information necessary to ascertain that person's safety to the head of the local government in which the child or young adult resides, and may request an on-site visit to the child or young adult's residence.
(3) Where any of the following circumstances is found as a result of an on-site visit under paragraph (2), the Minister of Health and Welfare may request that a telecommunications business operator under subparagraph 8 of Article 2 of the Telecommunications Business Act look up the telephone number of the child or young adult in crisis. In such cases, the relevant telecommunications business operator shall cooperate unless there are special circumstances:
1. Where direct contact with the person concerned cannot be made even through an on-site visit, and the person's safety cannot be ascertained even from statements by nearby residents or other persons;
2. Where it is suspected that a foul odor is emanating from the residence;
3. Other circumstances prescribed by Presidential Decree for ascertaining the safety of the person concerned.
(4) Matters necessary concerning the timing of requests for public data under paragraph (1), the retention periods for such data, and telephone number inquiries under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 25 (Policy Center for Children and Young Adults in Crisis)
(1) In order to develop policies for children and young adults in crisis and systematically manage the performance of dedicated support organizations nationwide, the Minister of Health and Welfare may designate an institution, organization, or corporation recognized as having the ability to perform such duties as a Policy Center for Children and Young Adults in Crisis (hereinafter referred to as the "Policy Center").
(2) The Policy Center shall perform the following duties:
1. Research, investigation, and planning concerning policies and systems relating to children and young adults in crisis;
2. Evaluation of the implementation results of the implementation plans of the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and local governments under Article 7 (2), and analysis and evaluation of the performance of duties by dedicated support organizations;
3. Administrative assistance necessary for the State and local governments to manage dedicated support organizations;
4. Support for the training and education of specialized personnel involved in policies and support programs for children and young adults in crisis;
5. Establishment of an online counseling and identification system, public outreach, and improvement of public awareness in order to establish a system for continuously identifying children and young adults in crisis;
6. Operation of the one-stop service channels for support for children and young adults in crisis, including online and telephone channels, under Article 10 (2);
7. Development of scientific scales for signs of social isolation or withdrawal under Article 20;
8. Operation of the certification system for institutions specializing in support for children and young adults in crisis under Articles 26 and 27;
9. Other projects necessary to achieve the objectives of policies and programs for children and young adults in crisis.
(3) Multiple institutions may be designated as Policy Centers according to the nature of the duties specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (2).
(4) The Minister of Health and Welfare may subsidize expenses necessary for the operation, etc. of the Policy Center within budgetary limits.
(5) Other matters necessary concerning the designation and operation, etc. of the Policy Center shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.

Article 26 (Certification of Specialized Institutions)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may certify any of the following corporations, institutions, or organizations that operate programs, etc. for children and young adults in crisis in an exemplary manner as an institution specializing in support for children and young adults in crisis:
1. A public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
2. A social welfare corporation under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Social Welfare Services Act;
3. A corporation established under the Civil Act or another Act.
(2) A corporation, institution, or organization seeking certification shall apply to the Minister of Health and Welfare as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) A corporation, institution, or organization certified pursuant to paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "specialized institution") may display a certification mark as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
(4) A corporation, institution, or organization that has not been certified pursuant to paragraph (1) shall not display a certification mark or any similar mark.
(5) The State and local governments may provide specialized institutions with necessary administrative and financial support, including housing support projects under Article 18, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(6) Other criteria and procedures for certification shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 27 (Period of Validity and Revocation of Certification)
(1) Certification under Article 26 shall be valid for three years from the date of certification. In such cases, matters necessary concerning extension periods, applications for extensions, etc. shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare.
(2) Where a specialized institution falls under either of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Health and Welfare may revoke its certification as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Health and Welfare: Provided, That certification shall be revoked in a case falling under subparagraph 1:
1. Where certification was obtained by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the institution no longer meets the certification criteria under Article 26 (6).
(3) The Minister of Health and Welfare shall not certify a corporation, institution, or organization whose certification has been revoked pursuant to paragraph (2) 1 if three years have not elapsed from the date of revocation.
(4) The Minister of Health and Welfare shall hold a hearing before revoking certification pursuant to paragraph (2).

CHAPTER 6 SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 28 (Pilot Projects)
(1) In order to effectively implement the dedicated support system for children and young adults in crisis, the Minister of Health and Welfare may conduct pilot projects where full implementation is expected to be difficult or where there is a need to verify methods of implementation, etc. in advance.
(2) Matters necessary concerning the participants, areas of implementation, methods, etc. of pilot projects under paragraph (1) shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Health and Welfare.

Article 29 (Protection of Personal Information)
(1) When collecting and managing information about children and young adults in crisis and their family members requiring care, the head of a dedicated support organization shall take necessary measures to prevent personal information from being lost, stolen, leaked, tampered with, or damaged.
(2) A person working for a dedicated support organization shall not use personal information about children and young adults in crisis and their family members requiring care learned in the course of their duties for improper purposes, including disclosing it without the data subject's consent, processing it without authority, or providing it for use by another person.
(3) When case management is terminated because a person eligible for support has achieved objectives such as returning to everyday life and becoming self-reliant, the head of a dedicated support organization shall, if the person so wishes, destroy the person's personal information and support history as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 30 (Delegation of Authority and Entrustment of Duties)
(1) The Minister of Health and Welfare may delegate part of the authority under this Act, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to Special Metropolitan City Mayors, Metropolitan City Mayors, Special Self-Governing City Mayors, provincial governors, Special Self-Governing Province Governors, mayors (including administrative mayors under Article 11 (1) of the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City), heads of counties, and heads of districts (meaning the heads of autonomous districts).
(2) The Minister of Health and Welfare may entrust part of the duties under this Act to institutions equipped with the personnel and facilities necessary to perform such duties, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER 7 PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 31 (Penalty Provisions)
A person who, in violation of Article 29 (2), uses personal information learned in the course of their duties for improper purposes, including disclosing it without the data subject's consent, processing it without authority, or providing it for use by another person, shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than five years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won.

Article 32 (Administrative Fines)
The Minister of Health and Welfare shall impose and collect an administrative fine not exceeding five million won, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, from a person falling under either of the following subparagraphs:
1. A person who obtains certification under Article 26 (1) by fraud or other improper means;
2. A person who displays a certification mark or any similar mark in violation of Article 26 (4).

ADDENDA <Act No. 20846, March 25, 2025>

Article 1 (Effective Date)
This Act shall enter into force one year after the date of its promulgation: Provided, That Article 24 shall enter into force two years after the date of its promulgation, and Article 28 shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation.

Article 2 (Preparatory Acts for the Enforcement of this Act)
From the date this Act is promulgated, the Minister of Health and Welfare may undertake preparatory acts necessary for its enforcement, including the formulation of the master plan, the conduct of fact-finding surveys, and the designation of dedicated support organizations and the entrustment of duties to them.
